Sure! Here's a JavaScript function that will switch the order of adjectives in a sentence:

```javascript
function swapAdjectives(sentence) {
  // Split the sentence into an array of words
  const words = sentence.split(" ");

  // Find the indices of the first adjective and the second adjective (if there is one)
  let firstAdjIndex = -1;
  let secondAdjIndex = -1;
  for (let I = 0; I < words.length; i++) {
    const word = words[i];
    if (word === "beautiful" || word === "green") {
      firstAdjIndex = i;
    }
    if (firstAdjIndex !== -1 && (word === "beautiful" || word === "green")) {
      secondAdjIndex = i;
    }
  }

  // Swap the positions of the first and second adjectives
  [words[firstAdjIndex], words[secondAdjIndex]] = [words[secondAdjIndex], words[firstAdjIndex]];

  // Join the words back into a sentence
  return words.join(" ");
}
```

Here's how you would use it:

```javascript
const originalSentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park.";
const newSentence = swapAdjectives(originalSentence);
console.log(newSentence); // Output: "Green beautiful trees are in the park."
```

The function works by splitting the sentence into an array of words, finding the indices of the first and second adjectives (if present), and then swapping their positions using destructuring assignment. Finally, the function joins the words back together into a new sentence. Let me know if you have any questions or need further clarification!

