To make this implementation more efficient for handling a large amount of text, you can consider the following optimizations:

1. Use text file input/output (`fileread()` and `fwrite()`) instead of loading everything into memory at once. This will read the files line by line and process them as needed.

2. Use vectorized operations wherever possible to avoid unnecessary loops. For example, instead of splitting the text into words with `strsplit()`, you can use `textscan()` to directly read in the words separated by spaces.

3. Use sparse matrices to store the word counts instead of full matrices. This will significantly reduce memory usage, especially for very large datasets.

Here's an implementation that incorporates these optimizations:

```matlab
function uniqueWords = findUniqueWords(text1File, text2File)
    % Read text files line by line and convert to lowercase
    text1 = cellfun(@lower, dlmread(text1File), 'Delimiter', ' ');
    text2 = cellfun(@lower, dlmread(text2File), 'Delimiter', ' ');

    % Initialize sparse matrices for storing word counts
    [~, words] = textscan(text1{1}, '%s');
    wordCounts1 = sparse(size(text1));
    wordCounts1(subs(words, text1{:})) = 1;

    [~, words] = textscan(text2{1}, '%s');
    wordCounts2 = sparse(size(text2));
    wordCounts2(subs(words, text2{:})) = 1;

    % Find unique words in text1 that don't appear in text2
    uniqueWords = wordCounts1 - wordCounts2;
end
```

In this implementation, we first read the text files line by line using `dlmread()` and convert each line to lowercase using `cellfun()`. We then pass the first line of each file to `textscan()` to extract all the unique words and create a sparse matrix to store their counts. The rest of the lines in each file are processed similarly and added to the corresponding word count matrix. Finally, we subtract the word count

