To handle this `InputMismatchException`, we can add some error handling to our program. Here's an updated version of the code with comments explaining what's changed:

```java
import java.util.*;

public class RetrieveInputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in); // Create a new Scanner object to read user input from standard input (the console)

        try {
            System.out.print("Enter the first number: "); // Prompt the user for the first number
            int num1 = input.nextInt(); // Read the first number as an integer and store it in the num1 variable

            System.out.print("Enter the second number: "); // Prompt the user for the second number
            int num2 = input.nextInt(); // Read the second number as an integer and store it in the num2 variable

            int sum = num1 + num2; // Calculate the sum of the two numbers

            System.out.println("The sum is: " + sum); // Display the calculated sum on the console
        } catch (InputMismatchException e) { // Catch any InputMismatchExceptions that may occur when reading user input
            System.err.println("Invalid input! Please enter integers only."); // Display an error message to the standard error stream (console red)
            input.nextLine(); // Consume any remaining input on the input buffer to prevent infinite loops
            main(args); // Recursively call the main method to prompt the user for valid input again
        }
    }
}
```

In this updated version, we wrap the entire block of code inside a `try` block, which catches any potential `InputMismatchException` thrown by the `nextInt()` method. If such an exception occurs, we display an error message to the standard error stream using `System.err.println()`. We then consume any remaining input on the input buffer using `input.nextLine()` to prevent infinite loops, and recursively call the `main()` method to prompt the user for valid input again. This helps ensure that our program handles invalid input gracefully and doesn't crash or produce unexpected results.

